\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{What the pretraining objective does not say}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{pretraining}
\tag{scaling-law}

\p{The definition in \ref{ftip-0005} combines the next-token objective with
the protocol that realizes it. This reflects two bodies of literature. The
Transformer and language-modelling literature supplies the likelihood
semantics; empirical scaling work such as \citef{kaplan2020scaling} and
\citef{hoffmann2022training} relates loss to model,
data, and compute under particular training families. Neither turns
next-token loss into an architecture-free theorem about later agentic
capability.}

\p{Pretraining also does not decide whether a later gain is elicitation or
acquisition. A successful trajectory may have tiny but nonzero base
probability, be reachable only under a different prompt or search budget, or
require feedback that introduces genuinely new task information. These cases
can have the same pretraining loss and different post-training curves.}

\p{This motivates two theorem families. A \strong{base-support} theorem should
bound what reward-only reweighting can achieve when no new informative event is
introduced. A \strong{conditional scaling} theorem should state explicitly
which base quantities—loss, success support, entropy, architecture efficiency,
or representation geometry—control the rate of post-training gain. The
literature suggests candidate coordinates; FTIP must prove which ones are
sufficient, necessary, or non-identifiable.}
